 second sound  it's in car and in city so each sound  has a word to help you understand how  that works let's go ahead and go through  the first eight just to understand that  a little bit better  so we looked at see let's look at the  phonogram Oh  so it has three sounds the first one is  ah and not the second is Oh n-no and the  third so if you see this funny double O  and you're wondering oh my goodness I  don't know what sound to tell my student  that is you have a word over here to  help you and you will know from the word  - that this double O signifies that the  sound is boo and for the letter G or the  phonogram G we have good and get and in  giant D did a has app in at a in eighth  and awe and talk so let's see that's one  so s will have as its first sound and  sit and lose enhance F and then I will  have it as in it I as in fine and E as  in machine so you have ass understanding  of each phonogram the sound that it  makes and then a word to help you  understand what that sound is the next  part I would like to share with you is  that your students have also learned how  to mark all of these sums within a word  so if you have a paper to pencil this is  the time where you are going to be  taking some notes so  students learn in addition to the sounds  and some of the definitions that go with  the sound they learn that they underline  phonograms of two or more letters so  they are gonna underline wherever they  find two or more phonograms together  they also learned that a phonograms  first sound is not marked so if it's if  the C is saying if the O is saying ah  if the G is saying it's the aliasing  yeah it's it's not marked in any way  it's just a known that it gets nothing  there so I would not put anything by  this o because it's saying its first  sound and not ah sounds other than first  sounds are numbered so in the example of  scent and great this C is going to get a  number 2 because it's the second sound  that you hear in that phonogram so  students 